          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-09370a771321\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Keith's “I love you, Nikki” is principally an avowal of his loving emotional state. It also reassures Nikki and supports his appeal for renewed contact, but neither reassurance nor the appeal establishes an independent commitment or relational undertaking. His concern and urgency describe the delivery and context, not expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The excerpt strongly suggests a voicemail or recorded phone message, though the precise medium is not explicitly named.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Keith's utterance is represented as direct quoted speech, apparently heard through a phone or recorded message; “Please, just...call me” suggests that Nikki is not participating in a live exchange.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Keith is distressed by Nikki's unexplained absence and uses the avowal amid concern, apology, and pleas for contact. The declaration functions as sincere-seeming reassurance and as emotional support for “Don't shut me out,” but the passage provides no positive evidence that it itself performs a commitment or erupts as a reflex-like discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you, Nikki.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal directed specifically to Nikki.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I woke up, and you were gone. Please, just...call me. I need to know you're okay.”\",\"supports\":\"Keith's concern and request for contact explain the declaration's reassuring and persuasive context without independently establishing performative commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“If I did something to upset you, I'm sorry. I love you, Nikki. Don't shut me out.”\",\"supports\":\"The apology and plea frame the avowal as an attempt to preserve communication and closeness; they do not supply evidence of reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Keith's voice came through” together with “call me”\",\"supports\":\"This supports classifying the represented event as quoted or revoiced speech, probably a phone message rather than a live exchange.\",\"confidence\":0.88}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The surrounding urgency, reassurance, apology, and appeal are contextual functions adequately described without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
